WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.160
Hi, I'm Nei Maldaner,

2
00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.060
and this is module eight of

3
00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:04.640
OSWork.

4
00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:05.780
In module seven,

5
00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:07.800
Denise built a restricted training bot,

6
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.560
but it only answers while her laptop

7
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:10.480
is on.

8
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:11.720
In this last module,

9
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:13.860
we'll move that bot off the laptop.

10
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.960
You'll understand what a VPS is and

11
00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:17.760
how big it needs to be,

12
00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.880
get into it over the internet without

13
00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:21.680
locking yourself out,

14
00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:23.420
install only what's needed,

15
00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.120
close the front desk and the password

16
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.240
file, hand the bot to a caretaker

17
00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.520
that starts and restarts it on its

18
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.720
own,

19
00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.580
and finally prove with a rehearsal that

20
00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:35.780
your backup comes back whole.

21
00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.620
You don't need to rent any machine

22
00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.460
right now: the practice works on your

23
00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:41.060
own computer or on paper.

24
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:42.340
When I ask,

25
00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:43.920
pause the video and do it right

26
00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.120
away.

27
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.220
Start with Denise's problem.

28
00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.740
Her training bot answers on Telegram only

29
00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.200
while the laptop is on.

30
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.160
At six in the evening she closes

31
00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.380
the lid, and the bot stops answering.

32
00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.400
The way out is a VPS.

33
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.620
Think of a rented room:

34
00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:00.880
the building owner hands you the room

35
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.600
with lights and a door,

36
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.520
and whatever happens inside is up to

37
00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:05.140
you.

38
00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.700
A VPS works the same way.

39
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:08.780
It's a rented virtual server,

40
00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.200
with memory, disk and network,

41
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.120
from a company called the VPS provider.

42
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:14.700
It runs all the time,

43
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.020
but users, updates and programs are your

44
00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:17.520
job.

45
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:18.700
On the laptop,

46
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.040
you were already taking care of all

47
00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.100
that without noticing.

48
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.380
On the VPS,

49
00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:23.340
you do it on purpose.

50
00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:24.840
Both work.

51
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.340
The VPS solves the closed-lid problem and,

52
00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:28.240
along with it,

53
00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:29.980
brings a new responsibility.

54
00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:31.980
So how big does this machine need

55
00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:32.900
to be?

56
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:34.880
Start small, with a machine that fits

57
00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.580
the program it will run.

58
00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:38.740
A bot that calls an AI model

59
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.020
through the API doesn't need an expensive

60
00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.580
graphics card, because the model runs on

61
00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:45.780
the AI provider's computers;

62
00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.540
the VPS only sends the request and

63
00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:49.800
receives the answer.

64
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.540
Lúcia almost rented a VPS with a

65
00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:54.480
graphics card for a bot with the

66
00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.360
class's fictional grade averages.

67
00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:57.880
The card would sit idle,

68
00:01:57.880 --> 00:01:59.215
and the bill would be high.

69
00:01:59.215 --> 00:02:01.475
Notice, too, that there are three separate

70
00:02:01.475 --> 00:02:03.495
bills: the VPS monthly fee,

71
00:02:03.495 --> 00:02:06.435
which arrives every month whether the machine

72
00:02:06.435 --> 00:02:07.975
is working or idle,

73
00:02:07.975 --> 00:02:09.155
the extra disk space,

74
00:02:09.155 --> 00:02:11.575
and the AI API.

75
00:02:11.575 --> 00:02:13.515
Paying for one doesn't pay for the

76
00:02:13.515 --> 00:02:14.015
others.

77
00:02:13.915 --> 00:02:15.815
It's the same logic as module one:

78
00:02:15.815 --> 00:02:17.675
each access has its own bill,

79
00:02:17.675 --> 00:02:18.875
and

80
00:02:19.075 --> 00:02:20.295
you write down the limit for each

81
00:02:20.295 --> 00:02:21.165
one.

82
00:02:21.165 --> 00:02:22.215
Before you rent,

83
00:02:22.215 --> 00:02:24.195
remember that a machine that's on doesn't

84
00:02:24.195 --> 00:02:25.735
mean a healthy service.

85
00:02:25.735 --> 00:02:27.995
The bot can stop in there without

86
00:02:27.995 --> 00:02:28.975
anyone noticing.

87
00:02:28.975 --> 00:02:32.015
So first decide what needs to run

88
00:02:32.015 --> 00:02:34.495
nonstop and who will check when something

89
00:02:34.495 --> 00:02:35.355
goes wrong.

90
00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:38.475
The course kit includes the file plano-vps.md,

91
00:02:38.475 --> 00:02:39.675
and

92
00:02:39.895 --> 00:02:42.695
its first part has six lines:

93
00:02:42.775 --> 00:02:43.975
the work,

94
00:02:43.975 --> 00:02:45.475
the technical owner,

95
00:02:45.475 --> 00:02:47.095
the VPS budget,

96
00:02:47.095 --> 00:02:48.955
the separate API budget,

97
00:02:48.955 --> 00:02:50.575
the system, which in the examples is

98
00:02:50.575 --> 00:02:51.475
Ubuntu,

99
00:02:51.475 --> 00:02:52.835
and the shutdown plan.

100
00:02:52.835 --> 00:02:54.875
For Denise, the last one took the

101
00:02:54.875 --> 00:02:57.315
longest: where the cancel button is and

102
00:02:57.315 --> 00:02:59.095
who can press it.

103
00:02:59.095 --> 00:03:00.695
Pause the video and fill in the

104
00:03:00.695 --> 00:03:02.335
six lines with a job of your

105
00:03:02.335 --> 00:03:02.835
own.

106
00:03:02.795 --> 00:03:04.715
Where data is missing,

107
00:03:04.715 --> 00:03:06.275
write to be defined and the name

108
00:03:06.275 --> 00:03:07.075
of whoever decides.

109
00:03:07.075 --> 00:03:08.055
Nothing

110
00:03:08.055 --> 00:03:09.875
gets rented, and the plan holds no

111
00:03:09.875 --> 00:03:11.635
password or credit card.

112
00:03:11.635 --> 00:03:13.095
With the room rented,

113
00:03:13.095 --> 00:03:14.735
the first door is the entrance.

114
00:03:14.735 --> 00:03:17.295
You get into the VPS through SSH,

115
00:03:17.295 --> 00:03:19.575
which creates a protected connection to manage

116
00:03:19.575 --> 00:03:20.695
the machine.

117
00:03:20.695 --> 00:03:22.795
The command is ssh,

118
00:03:22.895 --> 00:03:25.475
followed by the user,

119
00:03:25.475 --> 00:03:25.975
the at sign,

120
00:03:25.935 --> 00:03:28.075
and the VPS address.

121
00:03:28.155 --> 00:03:30.075
User and address are fields to replace,

122
00:03:30.075 --> 00:03:31.255
not real values,

123
00:03:31.255 --> 00:03:33.255
and the address shows up in the

124
00:03:33.255 --> 00:03:34.415
provider's panel.

125
00:03:34.415 --> 00:03:36.595
Lúcia opened two terminal windows and got

126
00:03:36.595 --> 00:03:38.755
mixed up: one was the laptop,

127
00:03:38.755 --> 00:03:39.995
the other was the VPS.

128
00:03:39.995 --> 00:03:42.615
What fixed it was looking at the

129
00:03:42.615 --> 00:03:43.695
start of the line,

130
00:03:43.695 --> 00:03:44.875
which, once you're in,

131
00:03:44.875 --> 00:03:47.355
shows the user and the VPS name,

132
00:03:47.355 --> 00:03:49.535
and running the pwd command,

133
00:03:49.535 --> 00:03:51.315
which tells you the folder you're in.

134
00:03:51.315 --> 00:03:52.695
So here's

135
00:03:52.895 --> 00:03:54.395
a habit for life:

136
00:03:54.395 --> 00:03:55.995
before any command,

137
00:03:55.995 --> 00:03:57.295
look at the start of the line.

138
00:03:57.295 --> 00:03:59.200
It tells you which machine you're on.

139
00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:00.340
To get in,

140
00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:02.580
use the public key registered the way

141
00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:04.120
the provider tells you.

142
00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.540
The private key never leaves your computer,

143
00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:08.420
and the provider's panel walks you through

144
00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:10.500
creating it when you sign up.

145
00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:11.800
On the first connection,

146
00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:14.260
SSH shows the machine's fingerprint and

147
00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:16.310
asks whether you want to continue.

148
00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.820
Denise compared that fingerprint with the one

149
00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.080
the panel showed,

150
00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:21.760
and only then typed yes.

151
00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.500
That question comes up once per machine;

152
00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:25.720
if it shows up again for the

153
00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:26.840
same VPS,

154
00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:28.540
stop and investigate.

155
00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:29.520
Inside,

156
00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:31.040
work with a user of your own,

157
00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:32.520
the work user.

158
00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:34.960
When a task needs administrator permission,

159
00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:36.700
put sudo in front of the command.

160
00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:38.880
That way, that power shows up only

161
00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.940
where you asked for it.

162
00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:42.980
And when you type the password,

163
00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:44.920
nothing appears on the screen.

164
00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:46.040
That's normal.

165
00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:47.800
Now, the rule that gives this lesson

166
00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:50.060
its name: don't return the old key

167
00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:52.060
before testing the new one.

168
00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:53.520
Before you change the network or the

169
00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:54.500
way you get in,

170
00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:56.440
leave the original session open,

171
00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:57.240
open another window,

172
00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:58.700
and test the new entry.

173
00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:00.580
Only close the first one when the

174
00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:01.200
second one works.

175
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.340
And check beforehand that the recovery console,

176
00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:05.540
in

177
00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:06.540
the provider's panel,

178
00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:10.069
opens the machine if everything fails.

179
00:05:11.069 --> 00:05:13.329
Pause the video and analyze a case

180
00:05:13.329 --> 00:05:13.829
on paper.

181
00:05:13.829 --> 00:05:14.909
Rogério,

182
00:05:14.909 --> 00:05:17.389
Denise's coworker, pasted a command from the

183
00:05:17.389 --> 00:05:19.129
internet that changes the SSH port,

184
00:05:19.129 --> 00:05:21.229
ran it, and closed the terminal right

185
00:05:21.229 --> 00:05:21.969
away.

186
00:05:21.969 --> 00:05:23.809
Now he can't get back in,

187
00:05:23.809 --> 00:05:25.749
and he never opened the console.

188
00:05:25.749 --> 00:05:27.189
Answer three questions:

189
00:05:27.189 --> 00:05:29.449
which rule was missing,

190
00:05:29.549 --> 00:05:31.169
where he can still get in,

191
00:05:31.169 --> 00:05:33.609
and what he writes in the plan.

192
00:05:33.609 --> 00:05:35.129
Then check the lesson's answer key.

193
00:05:35.129 --> 00:05:36.329
Inside,

194
00:05:36.629 --> 00:05:38.049
the machine comes almost empty,

195
00:05:38.049 --> 00:05:39.909
and the question becomes what to put

196
00:05:39.909 --> 00:05:40.409
in it.

197
00:05:40.169 --> 00:05:42.549
The course examples use Ubuntu,

198
00:05:42.549 --> 00:05:46.349
which installs and updates programs with apt.

199
00:05:46.749 --> 00:05:50.469
First, the command sudo apt update refreshes

200
00:05:50.469 --> 00:05:51.929
the list of what's available for the

201
00:05:51.929 --> 00:05:52.429
machine.

202
00:05:52.349 --> 00:05:53.549
Then,

203
00:05:53.609 --> 00:05:56.329
sudo apt upgrade proposes the updates and

204
00:05:56.329 --> 00:05:57.189
waits for your answer.

205
00:05:57.189 --> 00:05:58.929
At the end comes the question do

206
00:05:58.929 --> 00:06:00.049
you want to continue,

207
00:06:00.049 --> 00:06:01.949
with a capital Y as the default

208
00:06:01.949 --> 00:06:03.149
answer.

209
00:06:03.489 --> 00:06:04.849
On the practice VPS,

210
00:06:04.849 --> 00:06:06.569
Denise stopped at that question and read

211
00:06:06.569 --> 00:06:08.789
the whole list before answering Y.

212
00:06:08.789 --> 00:06:09.789
Do the same:

213
00:06:09.789 --> 00:06:11.189
if the list shows a program you

214
00:06:11.189 --> 00:06:12.369
don't recognize,

215
00:06:12.369 --> 00:06:13.769
look it up before you confirm.

216
00:06:13.769 --> 00:06:15.089
And the sudo in front,

217
00:06:15.089 --> 00:06:16.869
as you saw in the previous lesson,

218
00:06:16.869 --> 00:06:18.999
is the request for administrator permission.

219
00:06:18.999 --> 00:06:20.779
So what do you install?

220
00:06:20.779 --> 00:06:23.639
The course kit's bot needs Python 3,

221
00:06:24.359 --> 00:06:26.019
and Git helps you carry the project

222
00:06:26.019 --> 00:06:28.519
from your computer to the VPS.

223
00:06:28.599 --> 00:06:32.499
The command is sudo apt install git

224
00:06:32.499 --> 00:06:33.279
python3.

225
00:06:33.279 --> 00:06:34.899
The bot uses only what already comes

226
00:06:34.899 --> 00:06:37.359
with Python, with no extra add-ons.

227
00:06:37.359 --> 00:06:38.619
Other tools, like Codex,

228
00:06:38.619 --> 00:06:40.759
are optional and go in only if

229
00:06:40.759 --> 00:06:42.999
the program asks for them.

230
00:06:43.419 --> 00:06:45.519
A tutorial suggested that Lúcia install five

231
00:06:45.519 --> 00:06:46.919
tools, just to be safe.

232
00:06:46.919 --> 00:06:49.039
She checked what the bot needed and

233
00:06:49.039 --> 00:06:49.599
kept two.

234
00:06:49.599 --> 00:06:51.619
It's the carry-on bag logic:

235
00:06:51.619 --> 00:06:53.839
every extra program is one more thing

236
00:06:53.839 --> 00:06:55.519
to update and maintain.

237
00:06:55.519 --> 00:06:57.439
With five, that would be five things

238
00:06:57.439 --> 00:06:59.559
to update and three sitting unused.

239
00:06:59.559 --> 00:07:01.659
With two, both are at work,

240
00:07:01.659 --> 00:07:02.859
and

241
00:07:02.979 --> 00:07:04.619
the bot loses nothing it does.

242
00:07:04.619 --> 00:07:07.239
Before installing, check what's already there.

243
00:07:07.239 --> 00:07:08.999
Ask each program for its version,

244
00:07:08.999 --> 00:07:10.939
with python3 and the version option,

245
00:07:10.939 --> 00:07:12.559
and then with git and the same

246
00:07:12.559 --> 00:07:13.059
option.

247
00:07:12.979 --> 00:07:14.159
If it answers,

248
00:07:14.159 --> 00:07:15.239
it's on the machine.

249
00:07:15.239 --> 00:07:17.779
Next, create the projetos folder in your

250
00:07:17.779 --> 00:07:20.059
home folder with the mkdir command,

251
00:07:20.059 --> 00:07:22.699
and check it with ls.

252
00:07:23.419 --> 00:07:26.619
The tilde sign means my home folder.

253
00:07:26.619 --> 00:07:29.139
None of these commands changes the system.

254
00:07:29.139 --> 00:07:30.948
Lúcia ran all of it on her

255
00:07:30.948 --> 00:07:33.868
laptop before renting any VPS and wrote

256
00:07:33.868 --> 00:07:35.468
down the versions.

257
00:07:35.768 --> 00:07:37.188
Pause the video and do the same

258
00:07:37.188 --> 00:07:38.308
in your terminal;

259
00:07:38.308 --> 00:07:40.738
on Windows, use the WSL from module

260
00:07:40.738 --> 00:07:41.238
three.

261
00:07:40.908 --> 00:07:42.628
If you see command not found,

262
00:07:42.628 --> 00:07:44.188
it's not your mistake:

263
00:07:44.188 --> 00:07:46.508
note it as missing in the plan.

264
00:07:46.508 --> 00:07:48.388
Finally, write in the plan what the

265
00:07:48.388 --> 00:07:50.028
VPS needs to get:

266
00:07:50.028 --> 00:07:51.228
Python 3,

267
00:07:51.228 --> 00:07:54.228
Git, and nothing else without a reason.

268
00:07:54.248 --> 00:07:55.508
With the bot inside,

269
00:07:55.508 --> 00:07:57.148
the front desk is still missing.

270
00:07:57.148 --> 00:07:59.108
The firewall filters network connections,

271
00:07:59.108 --> 00:08:01.448
in both directions.

272
00:08:01.568 --> 00:08:03.308
It's like the school's front desk:

273
00:08:03.308 --> 00:08:04.848
there's a list of who can come

274
00:08:04.848 --> 00:08:06.468
in, and the mail carrier goes out

275
00:08:06.468 --> 00:08:07.048
to pick up the mail.

276
00:08:07.048 --> 00:08:10.888
The kit's bot works the second way,

277
00:08:10.888 --> 00:08:13.168
with what's called long polling:

278
00:08:13.168 --> 00:08:14.768
it goes out through a secure connection

279
00:08:14.768 --> 00:08:17.508
and asks Telegram whether a message has

280
00:08:17.508 --> 00:08:18.008
arrived.

281
00:08:17.868 --> 00:08:19.068
Nobody

282
00:08:19.488 --> 00:08:21.628
from outside needs to knock on its

283
00:08:21.628 --> 00:08:22.268
door.

284
00:08:22.268 --> 00:08:23.828
Lúcia thought she had to open a

285
00:08:23.828 --> 00:08:25.248
port so the bot could receive the

286
00:08:25.248 --> 00:08:26.748
class's messages.

287
00:08:26.848 --> 00:08:28.728
She didn't, because the only one who

288
00:08:28.728 --> 00:08:30.488
comes into the machine is her,

289
00:08:30.488 --> 00:08:31.708
through SSH.

290
00:08:31.708 --> 00:08:32.948
So keep the rule:

291
00:08:32.948 --> 00:08:34.248
with long polling,

292
00:08:34.248 --> 00:08:36.288
the only entrance the VPS needs is

293
00:08:36.288 --> 00:08:37.328
the SSH one.

294
00:08:37.328 --> 00:08:39.167
Everything else can stay closed.

295
00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:41.460
On Ubuntu, the firewall is set up

296
00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:42.100
with ufw.

297
00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:43.200
And

298
00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:44.560
the order matters:

299
00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:46.020
before turning it on,

300
00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:48.500
allow the SSH port your VPS actually

301
00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:49.580
uses.

302
00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:50.380
If it's twenty-two,

303
00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:53.020
the command is sudo ufw allow twenty-two

304
00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:54.520
slash tcp.

305
00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:55.800
If it's another one,

306
00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:57.160
the number changes.

307
00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:01.100
Only after that comes sudo ufw enable,

308
00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:03.240
which turns the firewall on.

309
00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:06.000
ufw itself warns you that this may

310
00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.540
drop open SSH connections,

311
00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:10.200
and asks whether to proceed.

312
00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:12.700
Denise only answered yes after setting up

313
00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:14.980
the ways back from the previous lesson:

314
00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:17.200
the second session tested and the provider's

315
00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:18.640
console checked.

316
00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:20.320
And there's one more detail:

317
00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:22.200
some providers have their own firewall in

318
00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:22.700
the panel.

319
00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:24.240
If yours does,

320
00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:26.340
check there too that the SSH port

321
00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:27.500
is allowed.

322
00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:30.040
What's left is the secrets file.

323
00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:31.060
The bot's token,

324
00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:33.020
its password on Telegram,

325
00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:35.200
lives in the dot env file.

326
00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:38.120
The command chmod six hundred makes that

327
00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:40.800
file readable only by its owner,

328
00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:42.520
and ls shows that at the start

329
00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:43.647
of the line.

330
00:09:43.647 --> 00:09:45.410
And dot env never goes into Git;

331
00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:47.550
the gitignore from module four already takes

332
00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:48.510
care of that.

333
00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:49.930
If the bot stops,

334
00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:52.530
don't open ports to see if it

335
00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:53.650
helps.

336
00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:55.410
Ask whether the process is running,

337
00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:57.230
whether the token was accepted,

338
00:09:57.230 --> 00:09:59.630
and whether outgoing network works.

339
00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:01.870
The log of Denise's bot said HTTP

340
00:10:01.870 --> 00:10:03.690
failure four oh one:

341
00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:04.910
it was the token,

342
00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:06.170
changed the week before.

343
00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:07.210
Pause

344
00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:07.910
the video and,

345
00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:09.170
in a practice folder,

346
00:10:09.170 --> 00:10:11.210
create an empty dot env,

347
00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:12.590
lock it with chmod,

348
00:10:12.590 --> 00:10:14.670
and check that the line starts with

349
00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:15.690
dash,

350
00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:16.890
r,

351
00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:18.270
w and only dashes.

352
00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:20.190
In the plan,

353
00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:21.710
write down the ports:

354
00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:23.090
the SSH one and,

355
00:10:23.090 --> 00:10:23.990
for the bot,

356
00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:24.770
none.

357
00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:26.350
The front desk is ready,

358
00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:27.850
but the bot still depends on you

359
00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:28.850
to start it.

360
00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:30.110
Up to now,

361
00:10:30.110 --> 00:10:31.530
you started the bot by hand,

362
00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:32.483
with

363
00:10:32.483 --> 00:10:34.550
python3 bot.py in the terminal.

364
00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:35.510
On the VPS,

365
00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:37.710
that ties the bot to your connection.

366
00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:40.190
Denise left the bot running in an

367
00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:42.050
SSH session and went home.

368
00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:43.810
The connection dropped on the way,

369
00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:45.390
and the bot stopped with it.

370
00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:48.130
What solves this is systemd,

371
00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:49.979
the machine's caretaker:

372
00:10:49.979 --> 00:10:52.219
it turns on the lights every morning,

373
00:10:52.219 --> 00:10:53.639
turns back on whatever went off,

374
00:10:53.639 --> 00:10:56.106
and writes everything down in the incident

375
00:10:56.106 --> 00:10:56.799
log book.

376
00:10:56.799 --> 00:10:57.899
With it,

377
00:10:57.899 --> 00:11:00.139
the bot starts by itself along with

378
00:11:00.139 --> 00:11:01.419
the machine,

379
00:11:01.419 --> 00:11:03.399
restarts fifteen seconds after a failure,

380
00:11:03.399 --> 00:11:04.599
and the record is kept for you

381
00:11:04.599 --> 00:11:05.279
to read later,

382
00:11:05.279 --> 00:11:07.179
instead of vanishing with the window.

383
00:11:07.179 --> 00:11:09.819
The instruction for this caretaker is

384
00:11:10.079 --> 00:11:12.339
a file called a unit,

385
00:11:12.339 --> 00:11:14.839
and the kit comes with one ready.

386
00:11:14.839 --> 00:11:17.999
The kit's unit is the file oswork-bot.service,

387
00:11:20.999 --> 00:11:22.099
and it says what,

388
00:11:22.099 --> 00:11:24.199
with whom, and where.

389
00:11:24.199 --> 00:11:26.179
Four fields take your user and your

390
00:11:26.179 --> 00:11:28.959
path: User, who runs the bot;

391
00:11:28.959 --> 00:11:31.049
WorkingDirectory, the folder;

392
00:11:31.049 --> 00:11:33.679
EnvironmentFile, where the dot env is;

393
00:11:33.679 --> 00:11:35.999
and ExecStart, the command that starts it.

394
00:11:35.999 --> 00:11:38.439
A fifth one you keep:

395
00:11:38.439 --> 00:11:40.739
Restart equals on-failure,

396
00:11:40.739 --> 00:11:43.579
which restarts it after a failure.

397
00:11:43.579 --> 00:11:44.979
On the VPS,

398
00:11:44.979 --> 00:11:47.099
the unit goes into the systemd folder,

399
00:11:47.099 --> 00:11:48.119
with sudo.

400
00:11:48.119 --> 00:11:49.099
Then

401
00:11:49.099 --> 00:11:51.079
daemon-reload makes systemd

402
00:11:51.079 --> 00:11:52.759
reread the units,

403
00:11:52.759 --> 00:11:54.929
enable with the now option starts the

404
00:11:54.929 --> 00:11:57.649
bot now and on every future reboot,

405
00:11:57.649 --> 00:12:00.009
and status shows its state.

406
00:12:00.409 --> 00:12:02.609
Denise saw active running.

407
00:12:02.609 --> 00:12:04.669
Even so, she only called the step

408
00:12:04.669 --> 00:12:07.909
done after sending slash status on Telegram

409
00:12:07.909 --> 00:12:09.849
and getting the answer.

410
00:12:09.969 --> 00:12:11.329
After starting it,

411
00:12:11.329 --> 00:12:12.849
do a restart on purpose,

412
00:12:12.849 --> 00:12:14.529
with systemctl restart,

413
00:12:14.529 --> 00:12:15.869
send slash status again,

414
00:12:15.869 --> 00:12:17.569
and write down the time.

415
00:12:17.569 --> 00:12:20.169
Next, read the record with the journalctl

416
00:12:20.169 --> 00:12:21.269
command.

417
00:12:21.269 --> 00:12:22.989
Lúcia saw the bot stop and come

418
00:12:22.989 --> 00:12:25.409
back there, with date and time.

419
00:12:25.409 --> 00:12:26.389
But watch out:

420
00:12:26.389 --> 00:12:28.209
restarting doesn't fix an error that keeps

421
00:12:28.209 --> 00:12:29.129
coming back.

422
00:12:29.129 --> 00:12:30.089
On another day,

423
00:12:30.089 --> 00:12:32.149
she saw the line process ended for

424
00:12:32.149 --> 00:12:33.289
diagnosis,

425
00:12:33.289 --> 00:12:34.049
stopped the service,

426
00:12:34.049 --> 00:12:35.789
and went looking for the cause before

427
00:12:35.789 --> 00:12:36.809
trying again.

428
00:12:36.809 --> 00:12:38.969
Now pause the video and adapt the

429
00:12:38.969 --> 00:12:40.969
kit's unit to your user.

430
00:12:40.969 --> 00:12:41.949
Unzip the kit,

431
00:12:41.949 --> 00:12:43.469
open oswork-bot.service

432
00:12:44.529 --> 00:12:46.589
with nano, replace the four fields with

433
00:12:46.589 --> 00:12:48.109
your user and path,

434
00:12:48.109 --> 00:12:49.249
save,

435
00:12:49.249 --> 00:12:51.469
and check with the grep command that

436
00:12:51.469 --> 00:12:53.309
none of the four lines still says

437
00:12:53.309 --> 00:12:54.509
oswork.

438
00:12:54.609 --> 00:12:55.289
Without a VPS,

439
00:12:55.289 --> 00:12:57.717
nothing starts: it's just a text file.

440
00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:01.320
Started and restarted still isn't care.

441
00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:03.180
systemd restarts the bot,

442
00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:04.900
but it doesn't warn anyone if the

443
00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:06.340
bot goes silent.

444
00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:08.960
That's why there's the external check:

445
00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:10.460
someone, outside

446
00:13:10.460 --> 00:13:12.020
the VPS, verifies

447
00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:13.580
that the service answers.

448
00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:16.420
A simple daily check records two things:

449
00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:18.240
whether the bot answered and how much

450
00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:19.840
disk space is left,

451
00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:21.120
which the df command shows on the

452
00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:22.100
VPS.

453
00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:23.920
Denise sends slash

454
00:13:24.020 --> 00:13:25.780
status from her phone every morning,

455
00:13:25.780 --> 00:13:27.860
before the eight o'clock meeting,

456
00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:28.860
and writes down the time of the

457
00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:29.860
answer;

458
00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:30.680
once a week,

459
00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:32.720
she also writes down the disk space.

460
00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:34.180
If your phone gets the answer,

461
00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:35.460
the whole path works.

462
00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:36.360
As for the disk,

463
00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:37.680
you look at it before it fills

464
00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:40.420
up, because a full disk makes

465
00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:42.020
any service stop.

466
00:13:42.020 --> 00:13:43.960
And the note carries a name,

467
00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:46.260
because the check belongs to someone.

468
00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:47.680
Now, the backups.

469
00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:49.280
Keep copies of the data outside the

470
00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:50.480
VPS,

471
00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:52.940
for example in a protected folder in

472
00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:54.580
the school's Drive

473
00:13:54.580 --> 00:13:55.980
or on an external disk.

474
00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:57.840
If the machine disappears,

475
00:13:57.840 --> 00:13:59.880
the copy can't go with it.

476
00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:01.340
Also set the retention,

477
00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:03.520
meaning how long each copy is kept.

478
00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:05.080
Lúcia used to keep the copy on

479
00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:07.220
the same VPS and started keeping it

480
00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:08.220
outside,

481
00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:09.985
with three monthly copies;

482
00:14:09.985 --> 00:14:10.600
the dot env,

483
00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:11.900
with the token,

484
00:14:11.900 --> 00:14:13.060
was left out.

485
00:14:13.060 --> 00:14:14.660
But a copy only counts after the

486
00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:16.540
rehearsal, like a fire drill.

487
00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:18.700
The test restores the file vendas.csv

488
00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:21.320
into a separate folder and compares it

489
00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:23.380
with the original using diff.

490
00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:24.900
If diff says nothing,

491
00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:25.980
the two are identical.

492
00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:27.920
In Denise's first test,

493
00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:28.800
the copy was empty:

494
00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:31.240
the command pointed to the wrong folder.

495
00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:33.120
The rehearsal found the error before a

496
00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:34.520
real loss.

497
00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:37.200
The course project closes with five pieces

498
00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:38.400
of evidence:

499
00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:39.600
authorized response,

500
00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:40.960
unknown user blocked,

501
00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:42.000
restart,

502
00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:42.813
log without token,

503
00:14:42.813 --> 00:14:44.593
and restore checked.

504
00:14:44.593 --> 00:14:47.053
Without a second Telegram account,

505
00:14:47.653 --> 00:14:50.853
the unknown-user block can be proven with

506
00:14:50.853 --> 00:14:52.393
the kit's self-test.

507
00:14:52.393 --> 00:14:54.173
And whatever wasn't done,

508
00:14:54.173 --> 00:14:55.153
you declare.

509
00:14:55.153 --> 00:14:56.733
Denise didn't have the VPS yet,

510
00:14:56.733 --> 00:14:58.913
so on the restart line she wrote:

511
00:14:58.913 --> 00:15:00.933
not executed,

512
00:15:00.933 --> 00:15:01.933
no VPS.

513
00:15:01.933 --> 00:15:04.173
A written not done is

514
00:15:04.393 --> 00:15:06.073
worth more than a done that nobody

515
00:15:06.073 --> 00:15:07.113
checked.

516
00:15:07.113 --> 00:15:09.273
Now pause the video and make a

517
00:15:09.273 --> 00:15:11.653
real backup on your computer:

518
00:15:11.653 --> 00:15:13.613
copy the kit's vendas.csv

519
00:15:13.713 --> 00:15:15.733
into a backup folder,

520
00:15:15.733 --> 00:15:17.973
restore it into a separate folder,

521
00:15:17.973 --> 00:15:19.053
and run diff.

522
00:15:19.053 --> 00:15:20.813
Silence is the sign you got it

523
00:15:20.813 --> 00:15:21.973
right.

524
00:15:21.973 --> 00:15:23.693
Then show the file with cat and

525
00:15:23.693 --> 00:15:25.013
add up the values:

526
00:15:25.013 --> 00:15:26.773
the total has to be one hundred.

527
00:15:26.773 --> 00:15:28.233
Write the date and the result in

528
00:15:28.233 --> 00:15:29.013
the plan.

529
00:15:29.013 --> 00:15:30.473
To wrap up:

530
00:15:30.473 --> 00:15:32.553
a VPS is a rented room:

531
00:15:32.553 --> 00:15:33.753
it runs all the time,

532
00:15:33.753 --> 00:15:35.633
and you're the one who takes care

533
00:15:35.633 --> 00:15:36.473
of it.

534
00:15:36.473 --> 00:15:38.293
Size follows the program,

535
00:15:38.293 --> 00:15:39.513
each bill has its limit,

536
00:15:39.513 --> 00:15:41.973
and the plan comes before the purchase.

537
00:15:41.973 --> 00:15:43.753
You get in through SSH,

538
00:15:43.753 --> 00:15:45.333
keeping the old key until the new

539
00:15:45.333 --> 00:15:47.413
one works, install only what the bot

540
00:15:47.413 --> 00:15:48.513
asks for,

541
00:15:48.513 --> 00:15:50.993
leave only the SSH port open,

542
00:15:50.993 --> 00:15:52.753
lock the dot env,

543
00:15:52.753 --> 00:15:55.013
and hand the bot to systemd,

544
00:15:55.013 --> 00:15:56.873
which starts, restarts

545
00:15:56.873 --> 00:15:57.393
and logs.

546
00:15:57.393 --> 00:15:58.333
And you prove,

547
00:15:58.333 --> 00:16:00.693
from outside, that it answers,

548
00:16:00.693 --> 00:16:02.053
and you rehearse the restore.

549
00:16:02.053 --> 00:16:03.093
With

550
00:16:03.093 --> 00:16:05.153
that, OSWork comes to an end.

551
00:16:05.153 --> 00:16:07.333
You leave with an organized folder with

552
00:16:07.333 --> 00:16:09.093
verifiable instructions,

553
00:16:09.273 --> 00:16:11.473
a Skill, the history in Git,

554
00:16:11.473 --> 00:16:12.873
a restricted bot,

555
00:16:12.873 --> 00:16:14.653
and a supervised operation plan on a

556
00:16:14.653 --> 00:16:15.293
VPS.

557
00:16:15.293 --> 00:16:16.453
From here on,

558
00:16:16.453 --> 00:16:18.013
the course becomes routine:

559
00:16:18.013 --> 00:16:19.153
the daily check,

560
00:16:19.153 --> 00:16:19.673
the monthly rehearsal,

561
00:16:19.673 --> 00:16:23.293
and the plan updated with every change.

562
00:16:23.393 --> 00:16:24.833
Put the next restore test on your

563
00:16:24.833 --> 00:16:27.073
calendar now, a month from today.

564
00:16:27.073 --> 00:16:29.053
Thank you for making it this far.
